# GDPR and LLMs: Art. 6, 9, and 32 in practice

**Disclaimer:** I'm a software engineer, not a lawyer. This post is a practical reading, not legal advice. For concrete compliance questions you need a data protection officer or a specialized law firm.

With that out of the way: three GDPR articles are particularly relevant when employees feed personal data into cloud LLMs like ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini.

## Art. 6: legal basis for processing

The moment PII lands in a prompt, processing in the GDPR sense begins. That requires a legal basis. The usual candidates:

**Consent (Art. 6(1)(a))** is difficult in an employment context, because the hierarchical relationship makes voluntariness questionable. Customer consents for "processing of your data by OpenAI in the US in order to reply to your email" are unrealistic — nobody clicks through that.

**Necessity for performance of a contract (Art. 6(1)(b))** doesn't work, because you could fulfil the contract with the customer without the LLM. The LLM is a convenience, not a necessity.

**Legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f))** is usually the only viable basis, but it requires a documented balancing test. That test has to show that your efficiency gain outweighs the data protection risks — which is a high bar when you're transferring data to a commercial provider in the US.

## Art. 9: special categories

If your prompt contains health data, biometric data, religious beliefs, trade union membership, or similarly sensitive categories, Art. 9 applies. The requirements are considerably stricter — the list of permissible processing grounds is shorter.

In practice that means: a lawyer sending a brief containing client data to ChatGPT has a concrete Art. 9 problem if the brief mentions health status or similar. An HR manager summarizing an application has one at the latest when the application contains disability ID numbers or similar special-category data.

## Art. 32: security of processing

Art. 32 requires "appropriate technical and organizational measures" for security. Among other things, the article explicitly mentions pseudonymization and encryption — both of which are countermeasures to exactly the risk LLM prompting creates.

Pseudonymization *before* handing data to a third party is an established TOM (technical/organizational measure). If you send only `[PERSON_1]`, `[EMAIL_1]`, `[IBAN_1]`, the third party is no longer processing personal data — they see no identifiable individuals.

This is where PII-masking tools become legally relevant. If you give employees a solution that lets them use cloud-LLM workflows without transmitting PII, you have implemented a concrete TOM.

## Processor relationship — or not?

A common misconception: "We have a DPA with OpenAI/Anthropic, so we're compliant." That's only partly true.

A data processing agreement (Art. 28) governs the relationship between the controller (you) and the processor (the LLM provider). It does not automatically make the processing itself lawful. You still need your own legal basis under Art. 6 (or 9), transfers to third countries need their own safeguards (Art. 44 ff.), and the data minimization principle in Art. 5(1)(c) applies regardless of the DPA.

Data minimization is interesting here: if the LLM provider can perform the task even without personal data (which is usually the case, since the LLM doesn't need real names to formulate language), the minimization principle obliges you not to transmit the data.

That's the legal consequence of the technical observation: an LLM can answer "write a polite reply to [CUSTOMER_1] regarding [ORDER_1]" just as well as the version with real names. If that's true, only the masked version belongs on the wire.

## What does this mean for companies?

Three pragmatic steps:

**1. Map the shadow IT.** First, understand which employees use which LLMs for what. Without that overview, you don't know where your risk lives.

**2. Provide a standard masking workflow.** If employees have an easy way to strip PII before sending, they'll use it. If the path is awkward or unclear, they'll keep going without. The tool has to fit into the natural workflow.

**3. Prefer browser-native tooling.** Server-side proxies that accept your prompt and mask it are themselves processors — which means you have to bring them under another DPA. Browser-native tools that send nothing outwards avoid that complication entirely. From a GDPR perspective, they're a substantially cleaner model.

## Where Redactly fits in

Redactly is explicitly designed to be usable as an Art. 32 TOM: no backend, no data transmission, full pseudonymization before anything leaves the browser. It's open source, so the property stays auditable. It can be self-hosted, so even delivery of the app itself can happen inside your own infrastructure.

That doesn't make Redactly a complete solution for LLM compliance — but it does make it a building block that solves a concrete part of the problem cleanly.

